# Linting Rules

This project uses `eslint-config-canonical` with strict rules. Follow these patterns to avoid common linting errors.

## Cyclomatic Complexity (max: 30)

Components with too many conditionals, hooks, or logic branches will exceed the complexity limit.

**Prevention Strategies:**

1. **Extract data fetching into custom hooks** - Move `useQuery`, `useFindMany`, and related logic into dedicated hooks:

```typescript
// ❌ Bad: All logic in one component
export function MyComponent() {
  const { data: session } = useSession();
  const { data: items, isLoading } = useItemQueries().useFindMany({...});
  const { data: related } = useRelatedQueries().useFindMany({...});
  // ... more queries and derived state
}

// ✅ Good: Extract to a hook
export function MyComponent() {
  const { items, related, isLoading, session } = useMyComponentData(id);
  // ... cleaner component
}
```

2. **Extract content into separate components** - When the main component has many conditional renders, extract the content:

```typescript
// ❌ Bad: Conditionals increase complexity
export function MyPage() {
  // ... many hooks and state
  if (loading) return <Loading />;
  if (!data) return <NotFound />;

  return (
    <div>
      {/* 100 lines of JSX with more conditionals */}
    </div>
  );
}

// ✅ Good: Extract the content
export function MyPage() {
  const { data, isLoading } = useData();

  if (isLoading) return <Loading />;
  if (!data) return <NotFound />;

  return <MyPageContent data={data} />;
}
```

3. **Use early returns** - Handle error states early to reduce nesting and conditionals.

## Non-null Assertions (`!`)

The `@typescript-eslint/no-non-null-assertion` rule forbids using `!` to assert non-null.

**Prevention Strategies:**

1. **Use optional chaining with truthiness checks:**

```typescript
// ❌ Bad: Non-null assertion
onClick={() => handleAction(item.nested!.id)}

// ✅ Good: Check exists before using
{item.nested?.id && (
  <Button onClick={() => handleAction(item.nested.id)}>
    Action
  </Button>
)}
```

2. **Narrow the type with a guard condition:**

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
const value = obj.optional!.property;

// ✅ Good
if (obj.optional) {
  const value = obj.optional.property;
}
```

3. **Destructure after checking:**

```typescript
// ✅ Good
const { nested } = item;
if (!nested) return null;
// Now `nested` is safely narrowed
```

## Identifier Length (min: 2 characters)

The `id-length` rule requires identifiers to be at least 2 characters.

**Common Violations:**

```typescript
// ❌ Bad: Single letter identifiers
items.map((i) => <Item key={i.id} item={i} />)
data.filter((d) => d.active)
reasons.map((r) => r.value)

// ✅ Good: Descriptive names
items.map((item) => <Item key={item.id} item={item} />)
data.filter((record) => record.active)
reasons.map((reason) => reason.value)
```

**Exceptions:** Loop indices (`i`, `j`) are sometimes acceptable in traditional for loops, but prefer descriptive names in callbacks.

## Module Sorting (perfectionist/sort-modules)

Types and functions must be sorted alphabetically within the file. The order is:

1. **Types** - Sorted alphabetically by name
2. **Export functions** - Sorted alphabetically  
3. **Private functions** - Sorted alphabetically

```typescript
// ❌ Bad: Types and functions out of order
type ZebraProps = { ... };
type AlphaProps = { ... };

export function MyComponent() { ... }

function zebra() { ... }
function alpha() { ... }

// ✅ Good: Alphabetically sorted
type AlphaProps = { ... };
type ZebraProps = { ... };

export function MyComponent() { ... }

function alpha() { ... }
function zebra() { ... }
```

**Callback Type Narrowing:** TypeScript doesn't narrow types inside callbacks. Extract to a component:

```typescript
// ❌ Bad: TypeScript can't narrow inside onClick callback
{item.nested?.id && (
  <Button onClick={() => handleClick(item.nested.id)}> // Error!
    Click
  </Button>
)}

// ✅ Good: Extract to a component where prop is already narrowed
<NestedButton nested={item.nested} onClick={handleClick} />

function NestedButton({ nested, onClick }: Props) {
  if (!nested) return null;
  return <Button onClick={() => onClick(nested.id)}>Click</Button>;
}
```

## Console Statements

Use `console.log` sparingly and with an eslint-disable comment:

```typescript
// eslint-disable-next-line no-console -- debugging purchase flow
console.log('Purchase data:', purchase);
```

## Summary Checklist

Before submitting code:

- [ ] Component complexity < 30 (extract hooks/components if needed)
- [ ] No `!` non-null assertions (use optional chaining + truthiness checks)
- [ ] All identifiers ≥ 2 characters (use descriptive names in callbacks)
- [ ] Types and functions sorted alphabetically within modules
- [ ] Properties sorted alphabetically in objects and JSX
- [ ] Extract components when TypeScript can't narrow types in callbacks
- [ ] No console.log without eslint-disable comment
